<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Red River Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1812 CE – 1870 CE · capital Upper Fort Garry</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A settlement founded by Lord Selkirk at the forks of the Red and Assiniboine rivers, cradle of the Métis nation and of the province of Manitoba.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1811 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-douglas-5th-earl-of-selkirk">Thomas Douglas</a>, Earl of Selkirk, about 300,000 square kilometres of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a> for a farming colony. Scottish settlers, many displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/highland-clearances">Highland Clearances</a>, arrived in 1812 under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miles-macdonell">Miles Macdonell</a>. The rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> allies saw the colony as a threat to the pemmican supply of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Conflict culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seven-oaks">Battle of Seven Oaks</a> in 1816, in which Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-semple">Robert Semple</a> and about twenty colonists were killed. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-douglas-5th-earl-of-selkirk">Selkirk</a>'s heirs sold it back to the Company in 1836, the district was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-assiniboia">Council of Assiniboia</a>. Its population was mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> and English-speaking country-born families living by farming, freighting and the buffalo hunt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the Company sold <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> without consulting residents, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a> formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a> in 1869. Negotiations led to the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> as a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1870.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/red-river-colony.jpg" alt="Red River Colony" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civil_ensign_of_the_United_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1812 CE – 1870 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Upper Fort Garry</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Miles Macdonell, Robert Semple</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French, Michif, Cree</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Canada</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Selkirk Settlement, Assiniboia</dd></dl></aside></div>

